# Template for this instance's PostgreSQL credential file (gitignored):
#
#   instances/policy_db/.env   the day-to-day role (policy_db_maintainer):
#                              owns the database, schemas, and tables; used by
#                              every ingest / embedding / validation run.
#
# This instance has exactly one credential file. The cluster's provisioning
# account lives at the workspace root as .env.provisioning, not here — it is not
# scoped to this instance (the same login provisions the engine's
# ingestion_test database and any future instance), so filing it under one
# instance would imply a boundary that does not exist. That account is not
# necessarily a superuser; see MAINTAINING.instance.policy_db.md §3 for what
# it must actually be able to do.
#
# Every entry point requires --env-file; there is no default credential file:
#   --env-file instances/policy_db/.env
#
# PERMISSIONS: the requirement is that no other local account can read a
# credential file. On a shared machine an unprotected .env.provisioning is
# readable by every local account, including the server's own `postgres`
# account. git will not save you here: these files are gitignored, so nothing
# in the repo ever inspects them.
#
# chmod 600 each credential file after creating or replacing it (a no-op on
# Windows, where the profile ACL already restricts it). On macOS the default
# umask (022) makes each new file world-readable, so re-apply by hand:
#
#   chmod 600 instances/policy_db/.env
#   chmod 600 .env.provisioning .env.test    # workspace-root cluster credentials
#   ls -l instances/policy_db/.env .env.provisioning .env.test   # -rw-------
#
# On Windows the profile ACL is the protection, so verify that instead — a
# mode check there reports something that means nothing:
#
#   icacls .env.provisioning    # owner + administrators only; no Users/Everyone
#
# All four variables are required.
POSTGRES_HOST=localhost
POSTGRES_PORT=5432
POSTGRES_USER=policy_db_maintainer
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=<password>
